# Copy to .env.local and edit. Vite only exposes variables prefixed with VITE_,
# and everything here is public — it reaches the browser and shows up in the
# built bundle. Nothing secret belongs in this file.
#
# Docker Compose reads .env (not .env.local) from this directory to fill in the
# web image's build arguments, so a Compose deployment wants these values in
# .env instead. Vite reads both, with .env.local winning, so keeping one of each
# is fine.

# The Go backend. Defaults to the address below, which matches server/.env.example's
# PORT, so running both halves locally needs no override.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8080

# GitHub OAuth app client ID. Public by design — it travels in the redirect URL.
# The matching client secret belongs in server/.env and nowhere near this file.
#
# Leave it empty and the app still works; the sign-in button explains that it is
# not configured, and the editor keeps saving to this browser as it always has.
#
# This must be the same OAuth app whose secret the backend holds. If the two
# drift apart, sign-in fails at the exchange step with "github did not accept
# this authorization code" — which reads like a stale code rather than a
# mismatch, so check this first.
VITE_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=

# Where GitHub sends the browser back, and what the OAuth app's "Authorization
# callback URL" must be set to. Defaults to this app's own origin — the site
# root, which is also the page a finished sign-in belongs on, because that is
# where the config library lives.
#
# Only set this when the app is served from a subpath, and make sure whatever
# serves the built files answers that path — and /editor — with index.html.
# VITE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/
